# Scenario for scripts/record-scenario.py: mandelbrot.inc's loop_i does 32-bit
# sign-extend/multiply/divide/subtract through DEHL, an instruction class
# the Z80 has none of. Watch one frame render, disassemble that block,
# single-step it on real hardware, then enable the cache and run again.
#
# ADDRESSES ARE PINNED TO THE CURRENT samples/z280/mandelbrot.hex: 1B1 is
# loop_i's first instruction (12 of them, ending in subw at 1D4), 145 is
# the `call newline` right after mandelbrot() returns -- once per frame,
# so a breakpoint there turns every 'G' into "run exactly one frame". If
# mandelbrot.asm/.inc/.hex ever change, re-check both against the fresh
# mandelbrot.lst before recording -- a stale address will look like it
# works (it disassembles *something*) while showing the wrong thing.

# Open on the help screen, so a viewer knows the command set before
# anything starts moving.
key ?
pace 2.0

key R
upload samples/z280/mandelbrot.hex

# A breakpoint right after each frame finishes drawing: every 'G' from
# here on runs exactly one frame, no timing guess, no halt port.
type B
wait Break addr?
command 148
pace 0.6

# The block that makes the case: exts/divw/multw/subw, all DEHL-wide.
type D
wait Disassemble?
command 1B4
pace 1.2

# Same block, single-stepped on the chip -- a short hop from here, deep
# in the frame that just finished, to the next frame's first pixel.
type g
wait Go until?
command 1CD 4
pace 0.9
step 8
pace 1.2

# Watch one frame render live, uncached.
command G 10
pace 1.2

# Enable the instruction and data cache, then one more frame -- the
# breakpoint at 145 survives the reset, so 'G' alone is enough.
key R
type =
wait Set register?
command IC 1
type =
wait Set register?
command DC 1
pace 0.6
command G 0
pace 0.9
